BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Reed College": "https://events.reed.edu/event/the-musician-as-philosopher-listening-to-alice-coltrane"}
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Reed College": "https://events.reed.edu/event/music-as-expenditure-state-terror-and-the-truths-of-music"}
X-SOURCE-ID:reed_college
X-SOURCE:Reed College
CATEGORIES:
DESCRIPTION:This talk explores the fast-evolving relationship between art a
 nd politics in the 21st century\, using the 2026 ICE occupation in Minneapo
 lis as a prism. It argues that 20th century Western style history—based in 
 the teleological pursuit of formal novelty—has not simply been exhausted\; 
 it has now reached a state of saturation. This saturation is evidenced by d
 ata showing a marked decline in new musical genres since 2000\, as digital 
 platforms transition into what Cory Doctorow calls an "enshittified" politi
 cal economy of stagnant monopolies and AI-generated content. Drawing on Geo
 rges Bataille’s theory of expenditure\, the talk posits that the power of a
 rt is shifting away from formal innovation toward reenchanted forms of soci
 al practice. By linking Jacques Attali’s concepts of noise and sacrifice to
  the metaphysical impact of witnessing state terror\, it argues that music 
 today may be disclosing its truths through instances of purposeless\, non-e
 conomic expenditure.\n\nSponsored by The Evelyn Herold Colloquium Series in
  Humanities and the Film and Media Studies program. Free and open to the pu
 blic.
DTEND:20260417T010000Z
DTSTAMP:20260403T022346Z
DTSTART:20260417T000000Z
LOCATION:Psych 105
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:The Evelyn Herold Colloquium Series in Humanities Presents: "Music 
 as Expenditure: State Terror and the Truths of Music"
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_52399890987287
URL:https://events.reed.edu/event/music-as-expenditure-state-terror-and-the
 -truths-of-music
END:VEVENT
