BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Lewis Clark": "https://www.lclark.edu/calendars/events/event/371530-fat-studies-health-at-every-size-online"}
X-SOURCE-ID:lewis_clark
X-SOURCE:Lewis Clark
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260425T090000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260425T173000
LOCATION:Online
SUMMARY:Fat Studies & Health at Every Size (Online)
DESCRIPTION:This course explores the interdisciplinary field of scholarsh
 ip in Fat Studies that aims to debunk weight-centric misconceptions and c
 ountering mainstream narratives.  Participants will examine fat bias\, fa
 t shame and weight-based oppression as a social justice issue that inters
 ects with other systems of oppression.  This course includes an introduct
 ion to the paradigm of Health At Every Size (HAES)\, a weight-neutral app
 roach to health promotion that emphasizes size diversity\, intuitive eati
 ng\, and joyful movement. HAES is also an important paradigm in working w
 ith people who experience disordered eating.  This course is part of our 
 Eating Disorders Certificate Program (https://graduate.lclark.edu/program
 s/continuing_education/certificates/eating_disorders/).&#160\;Course Deta
 ils &amp\; RegistrationDates &amp\; Times: Saturday-Sunday\, April 25-26\
 , 2026\, 9 a.m.-5:30 p.m.\, PT\, OnlineInstructor: Krystal Marcinkiewicz\
 , MA\, LPC\, LMHCCurrent Students and Certificate Candidates: CECP 835-02
 \, 1 semester hour of Graduate Continuing Education Credit\, $375. Contac
 t ccecerts@lclark.edu (mailto:ccecerts@lclark.edu) with questions. This s
 ection is currently full\, join the waitlist (https://docs.google.com/for
 ms/d/e/1FAIpQLScdcTBjWzPfuNP8ikLCYpsXPrXLkTsvU5pmoYUhw2Akn164rA/viewform)
  to be notified if a spot opens up.Individual Class: We welcome students 
 seeking to take individual courses within the certificate program. Admiss
 ion to the program is required. Click here for more information (https://
 www.lclark.edu/programs/continuing_education/certificates/eating_disorder
 s/#Admission)We are committed to making our events accessible to all need
 s and abilities. If you have a disability that may impact your academic p
 erformance\, you may request accommodations by meeting with the Office of
  Student Accessibility staff and submitting documentation on the Office o
 f Student Accessibility website (/offices/student-accessibility/). Please
  be aware that the process for evaluation and implementation of accommoda
 tions could take 2-3 weeks\, so please be in touch with the OSA as soon a
 s possible. Email access@lclark.edu (mailto:access@lclark.edu) with any a
 dditional questions or concerns.About the InstructorKrystal Marcinkiewicz
 \, MA\, LPC\, LMHC is a Licensed Professional Counselor and Clinical Supe
 rvisor\, having earned her Masters Degree in Professional Mental Health C
 ounseling from Lewis &amp\; Clark College in 2018. Drawing from an integr
 ative feminist approach\, her work is grounded in person-centered values\
 , emphasizing authentic connection\, and collaborative engagement. Her pr
 imary clinical focus is supporting clients in navigating identity develop
 ment\, body politics\, neurodivergence\, emotional codependency\, highly 
 sensitive personalities and other complex emotional experiences.  In addi
 tion to clinical work\, Krystal is also passionate in training and mentor
 ing emerging counselors. She is an adjunct professor\, teaching a wide va
 riety of graduate counseling courses with an underlying emphasis on couns
 elor identity development\, reflective practice\, and cultivating profess
 ional resilience.  In both the classroom\, and her clinical work\, Krysta
 l is committed to creating affirming spaces that honor intersectional ide
 ntities and lived experience.New workshops and trainings are added to our
  calendar regularly. For the latest on professional development related t
 o your specific interests\, sign up for our mailing list! (https://lclark
 .tfaforms.net/4735441)
