 6\, 9 a.m.- 4 p.m.\, Clackamas Education Service District\, 13455 SE 97th
  Avenue\, Clackamas\, OR&#160\;</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\; padding-l
 eft: 40px\;"><strong>Virtual:&#160\;</strong>June 23\, 25\, 2026\, 9:30-1
 1:30 a.m.</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Instructors:</strong> 
 Elizabeth Denevi\, PhD\, and Jasmine Locke</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\
 ;"><strong>Graduate Continuing Education credit:</strong> CEED-866-21\, 2
  semester hours\, $770</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Limited s
 cholarships available</strong> to educators working in a Multnomah or Cla
 ckamas County School District\, MESD\, or CESD. All course texts and mate
 rials will be provided to all participants at no cost.</p><h2 style="line
 -height: 1.5\;">  Course Registration</h2><p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Fol
 low the link below to access registration and scholarship application</p>
 <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> CCE will follow up by email with credit-beari
 ng course registration and enrollment instructions</p><p style="line-heig
 ht: 1.5\;"><strong><a class="btn-small-style-1" href="https://pdnetworks.
 soesd.k12.or.us/public/events/view-event/15508" target="_blank" rel="noop
 ener">Register online</a></strong></p><hr /><h2><strong>About the Instruc
 tors</strong></h2><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Elizabeth Denevi\
 , PhD\,</strong> is an assistant clinical professor at Lewis &amp\; Clark
  Graduate School of Education and Counseling. She has served as a classro
 om teacher as well as an equity director\, leadership coach\, and directo
 r of professional development. She is co-author of <em>Learning and Teach
 ing While White: Antiracist Strategies for School Communities</em> (with 
 Jenna Chandler Ward)&#160\;as well as <em>Integrating Educator Well-Being
 \, Growth\, and Evaluation: Four Foundations for Leaders</em> (with Lori 
 Cohen)\, both featured titles in Routledge's Equity and Social Justice in
  Education series.</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Jasmine Locke
 </strong> is an equity-centered educational leader and doctoral candidate
  at Lewis &amp\; Clark Graduate School of Education and Counseling. Her r
 esearch explores racial literacy\, socialization\, and the educational tr
 ajectories of students engaged in Black Student Unions. With a decade of 
 experience as a classroom teacher\, school support specialist and equity 
 coach\, she brings deep expertise in culturally responsive pedagogy\, stu
 dent identity development\, and systems-level change. She is featured in 
 <em>Teach for Climate Justice: A Vision for Transforming Education</em>\,
  highlighting her commitment to joy as a form of resistance.</p><div id="
 lw_widget_470b39ba" class="lw_widget lw_widget_type_blurbs lw_widget_blur
 bs lw_widget_id_3994 lw_widget_opt_in lw_widget_pagination"><ul class="lw
 _widget_results lw_widget_results_blurbs"><li class="lw_item_1"><div clas
 s="lw_blurbs_body"><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><em><strong>New workshop
 s and trainings are added to our calendar regularly.</strong> For the lat
 est on professional development related to your specific interests\, <a h
 ref="https://lclark.tfaforms.net/4735441">sign up for our mailing list!</
