BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Lewis Clark": "https://www.lclark.edu/calendars/events/event/387963-treating-the-what-ifs-understanding-applying-exposure"}
X-SOURCE-ID:lewis_clark
X-SOURCE:Lewis Clark
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260805T090000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260805T130000
LOCATION:Online
SUMMARY:Treating The "What Ifs": Understanding & Applying Exposure & Resp
 onse Prevention (ERP) [Online]
DESCRIPTION:What if I embarrass myself? What if I get sick? What if I sen
 t the wrong person an email? What if I caused a car accident?  Anxiety is
  a natural\, human response that no one is immune to experiencing. So whe
 n does anxiety turn from typical to disorder\, and from adaptive into mal
 adaptive?  On average\, folks with OCD suffer 17 years before receiving c
 orrect diagnosis and effective treatment. It is vital for clinicians to b
 ecome skilled at diagnosis and effective treatments such as Exposure and 
 Response Prevention (ERP) to ensure there is no delay in recovery.  For d
 ecades\, ERP has been one of the most studied and effective therapies to 
 treat anxiety disorders and OCD. When applied well\, ERP can save lives\,
  foster resilience\, and lead to recovery. Many clinicians are unsure or 
 even fearful of learning this modality because of misunderstanding the tr
 eatment model (example: that ERP is cruel\, or further traumatizing).  ER
 P treatment targets reducing behaviors that get in the way of the life th
 e person wants to live\, increases tolerance of distressing feelings and 
 empowers clients through self-efficacy.  This workshop provides an in-dep
 th view of Exposure &amp\; Response Prevention\, and modern perspectives 
 on application.&#160\;Following this training\, participants will have a 
 better ability to:      Better assess and differentiate diagnosis        
   Understand the principles of ERP          Identify response prevention 
 techniques          Develop an exposure hierarchy in line with treatment 
 protocols.    Details &amp\; RegistrationDate and Time: Wednesday-Thursda
 y\, August 5-6\, 2026\, 9 a.m.-1 p.m. PT\, OnlineInstructor: Sarah Weber\
 , MA\, LMHC\, LPC\, ATRCost and Credits: $160 before 7/5\, $185 after. In
 cludes 7 CEUs. GSEC Alumni and Adjuncts save 20%. $75 Student Rate.Regist
 er Online (https://lclark.tfaforms.net/wf1W4MQ?epid=a1aUY000002VaIz)Regis
 tration for this workshop will close after Sunday\, August 5thAccessibili
 ty: We are committed to making our events accessible to all needs and abi
 lities. When registering\, please let us know your access needs. Contact 
 us at 503-768-6040 or cce@lclark.edu (mailto:cce@lclark.edu)&#160\;with q
 uestions.Please note: Discounted &#x2018\;Student Rate&#x2019\; registrat
 ions are for current students only and do not include continuing educatio
 n credit (CEU/PDUs)About the InstructorSarah Weber\, MA\, LMHC\, LPC\, AT
 R (she/her) is an artist\, art therapist\, and mental health counselor in
  Oregon and Washington states. She works with folks of all ages who are l
 iving with anxiety disorders\, OCD\, and trauma using both evidence-based
  practices (ACT\, ERP and I-CBT) and expressive therapies. Sarah has work
 ed in various settings\, from schools to higher levels of care\, and is n
 ow in private practice. She is on the board of OCD Washington and hosts a
 rt-making events to create community. Outside of work\, she enjoys practi
 cing yoga and spending time with her dog outdoors. Information about Sara
 h's practice can be found here (http://www.sarah-weber.org).New workshops
  and trainings are added to our calendar regularly. For the latest on pro
 fessional development related to your specific interests\, sign up for ou
 r mailing list! (https://lclark.tfaforms.net/4735441)
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><em>What if</e
 m> I embarrass myself? <em>What if</em> I get sick? <em>What if</em> I se
 nt the wrong person an email? <em>What if</em> I caused a car accident?</
 p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;">  Anxiety is a natural\, human response t
 hat no one is immune to experiencing. So when does anxiety turn from typi
 cal to disorder\, and from adaptive into maladaptive?</p><p style="line-h
 eight: 1.5\;">  On average\, folks with OCD suffer 17 years before receiv
 ing correct diagnosis and effective treatment. It is vital for clinicians
  to become skilled at diagnosis and effective treatments such as Exposure
  and Response Prevention (ERP) to ensure there is no delay in recovery.</
 p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;">  For decades\, ERP has been one of the m
 ost studied and effective therapies to treat anxiety disorders and OCD. W
 hen applied well\, ERP can save lives\, foster resilience\, and lead to r
 ecovery. Many clinicians are unsure or even fearful of learning this moda
 lity because of misunderstanding the treatment model (example: that ERP i
 s cruel\, or further traumatizing).</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;">  ER
 P treatment targets reducing behaviors that get in the way of the life th
 e person wants to live\, increases tolerance of distressing feelings and 
 empowers clients through self-efficacy.</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;">
   This workshop provides an in-depth view of Exposure &amp\; Response Pre
 vention\, and modern perspectives on application.&#160\;</p><p><strong>Fo
 llowing this training\, participants will have a better ability to:</stro
 ng></p><ul><li><p style="line-height: 1.6\;">      Better assess and diff
 erentiate diagnosis    </p></li><li><p style="line-height: 1.6\;">      U
 nderstand the principles of ERP    </p></li><li><p style="line-height: 1.
 6\;">      Identify response prevention techniques    </p></li><li><p sty
 le="line-height: 1.6\;">      Develop an exposure hierarchy in line with 
 treatment protocols.    </p></li></ul><hr /><h2><strong>Details &amp\; Re
 gistration</strong></h2><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Date and Ti
 me:</strong> Wednesday-Thursday\, August 5-6\, 2026\, 9 a.m.-1 p.m. PT\, 
 Online</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>Instructor:</strong> Sara
 h Weber\, MA\, LMHC\, LPC\, ATR</p><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>
 Cost and Credits:</strong> $160 before 7/5\, $185 after. Includes 7 CEUs.
  GSEC Alumni and Adjuncts save 20%. $75 Student Rate.</p><p style="line-h
 eight: 1.5\;"><strong><a class="btn-small-style-1" href="https://lclark.t
 faforms.net/wf1W4MQ?epid=a1aUY000002VaIz">Register Online</a></strong></p
 ><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><em>Registration for this workshop will cl
 ose after Sunday\, August 5th</em></p><div id="lw_widget_30f0017c" class=
 "lw_widget lw_widget_type_blurbs lw_widget_blurbs lw_widget_id_7119 lw_wi
 dget_accessibility_statement lw_widget_pagination"><ul class="lw_widget_r
 esults lw_widget_results_blurbs"><li class="lw_item_1"><div class="lw_blu
 rbs_body"><p class="p1" style="font-size: 15px\; line-height: 1.5\;"><em>
 <strong>Accessibility:</strong> We are committed to making our events acc
 essible to all needs and abilities. When registering\, please let us know
  your access needs. Contact us at 503-768-6040 or <a href="mailto:cce@lcl
 ark.edu">cce@lclark.edu</a>&#160\;with questions.<br/></em></p><p class="
 p1" style="font-size: 15px\; line-height: 1.5\;"><em><strong>Please note:
 </strong> Discounted &#x2018\;Student Rate&#x2019\; registrations are for
  current students only and <strong>do not</strong> include continuing edu
 cation credit (CEU/PDUs)</em></p></div></li></ul></div><hr /><h2><strong>
 About the Instructor</strong></h2><p style="line-height: 1.5\;"><strong>S
 arah Weber\, MA\, LMHC\, LPC\, ATR</strong> (she/her) is an artist\, art 
 therapist\, and mental health counselor in Oregon and Washington states. 
 She works with folks of all ages who are living with anxiety disorders\, 
 OCD\, and trauma using both evidence-based practices (ACT\, ERP and I-CBT
 ) and expressive therapies. Sarah has worked in various settings\, from s
 chools to higher levels of care\, and is now in private practice. She is 
 on the board of OCD Washington and hosts art-making events to create comm
 unity. Outside of work\, she enjoys practicing yoga and spending time wit
 h her dog outdoors. Information about Sarah's practice <a href="http://ww
 w.sarah-weber.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">can be found here</a>.<
 /p><div id="lw_widget_470b39ba" class="lw_widget lw_widget_type_blurbs lw
 _widget_blurbs lw_widget_id_3994 lw_widget_opt_in lw_widget_pagination"><
